 "answer": "Kuala Lumpur's air quality is currently poor, at the \"Unhealthy\" to \"Very Unhealthy\" level. I got these figures from search results that were cached at different times, so they don't match exactly.\n\n| Source | US AQI | Category |\n|---|---|---|\n| IQAir (Oct 16, 12:00 local) | 207 | Very Unhealthy |\n| AQICN (updated Thursday 16:00) | 207 | Very Unhealthy |\n| AQI.in (Oct 15, 20:31) | 176 | Unhealthy |\n| AccuWeather (Oct 15) | 155 | Very Unhealthy (its own label) |\n\n- **Main pollutant:** The main pollutant is fine particulate matter (PM2.5). AQI.in reported about 104 µg/m³ of PM2.5 and 136 µg/m³ of PM10. Nitrogen dioxide and sulfur dioxide were very low.\n- **Forecast:** IQAir's hourly forecast showed the AQI easing through the afternoon and evening, from about 207 to the low 100s by late evening.\n- **Station differences:** Readings vary across the city. AQICN's map showed stations such as Cheras and Batu Muda in the 160s.\n\nAt these levels, sensitive groups (children, older adults, and people with asthma or heart or lung conditions) should avoid prolonged outdoor activity. Everyone else should limit strenuous exertion outdoors. A well-fitted N95 or KN95 mask helps outdoors, and keeping windows closed or using an air purifier helps indoors.\n\nFor a live reading, check iqair.com or aqicn.org, or the Malaysian Department of Environment's API system. I can't see a real-time feed.",
